</section>
<section id="A1.SS5.SSS7" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">A.5.7 </span>Impact of Occlusion Filtering on Model Performance</h4>

<div id="A1.SS5.SSS7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS5.SSS7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the aggregated gaze model, gaze points are collected within a specified time interval <math id="A1.SS5.SSS7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> (200 ms). However, in dynamic environments, aggregating gaze points from the interval <math id="A1.SS5.SSS7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[t-\delta,t]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[t-\delta,t]</annotation></semantics></math> may introduce inaccuracies due to changes in the scene or camera movement. To mitigate this, we introduced an occlusion check to ensure that only relevant gaze points are aggregated. More details about the occlusion check method can be found in Sec. <a href="#A1.SS3" title="A.3 Occlusion-Check Process ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Gaze-VLM: Bridging Gaze and VLMs via Attention Regularization for Egocentric Understanding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.3</span></a> in the Appendix.
To evaluate the impact of this adjustment, we conducted experiments comparing models with and without the occlusion check in the future prediction task. As shown in Table <a href="#A1.T12" title="Table 12 ‣ A.5.7 Impact of Occlusion Filtering on Model Performance ‣ A.5 Other Experiments ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Gaze-VLM: Bridging Gaze and VLMs via Attention Regularization for Egocentric Understanding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>, the openflamingo implementation of our framework incorporating the occlusion check slightly outperforms the one without it. The difference in the evaluation metrics can be attributed to the fact that only relevant and accurate gaze points are considered, which reduces noise and prevents the model from being confused by irrelevant data.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.T12" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 12: </span>Effect of using occlusion filtering during training</figcaption>
